//
// A text field or element handed to a `&text` parameter is refused, naming the cure: a text
// place is a string record in a store, and a `&text` parameter reaches only a text variable, so
// the call could only hand the callee a copy — and its write would be lost.
// `(B-Ref-Reshape)` refuses a link it cannot honour rather than downgrade it to a copy.
//
// Before loft#1602 both backends compiled `app(o.s)` with `fn app(t: &text) { t = t + "!" }`
// and left `o.s` unchanged: a silent lost write.  This is the stopgap; @PLN167 C3 makes the
// same call link to the place.
//
// A text VARIABLE still links, and a temporary (a literal, a computed text) still takes a
// read-only work copy — nothing names it, so nothing can miss the write.  The standard
// library's `directory("sub")` relies on that.  Neither may be refused.
//
// Every expected error below is matched exactly once.
//
// @falsified-at: 92bdca3d9 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/3 -> 0/3 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/3 -> 0/3 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1602-a-text-place-to-a-ref-text-parameter-is-refused.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/3 -> 0/3 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/3 -> 0/3 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// HOLDS: leak, panic and free-refusal are clean on both trees and both backends.
//
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostics, one per refused text place.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a `&text` parameter cannot link to a text field or element
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a `&text` parameter cannot link to a text field or element
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a `&text` parameter cannot link to a text field or element

struct O { s: text }

fn app(t: &text) { t = t + "!"; }
fn via_ref(q: &O) { app(q.s); }

fn main() {
  o = O { s: "cc" };
  app(o.s);
  v: vector<text> = ["a"];
  app(v[0]);
  loc = "x";
  app(loc);
  app("lit");
  d = directory("sub");
  via_ref(o);
  println("{o.s} {v} {loc} {len(d) > 0}");
}
